{#
  Public-facing layout for the provider search.

  Extends the helpers bundle's public layout, which in turn extends the
  application's base.html.twig — so the app keeps final say over <head>, the
  importmap and any global chrome, exactly as it does for the admin screens.

  Nothing here requires JavaScript. The search is an HTML form and a set of
  links; if the importmap never loads, every feature on the page still works.
#}
{% extends '@PixiekatSymfonyHelpers/layout.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}{% block search_page_title %}Find a provider{% endblock %} · HMFP{% endblock %}

{% block metas %}
  {{ parent() }}
  <meta name="content-language" content="en">
  <meta name="description" content="Search for physicians, facilities, and departments in the HMFP provider directory.">
{% endblock %}

{% block stylesheets %}
  {{ parent() }}
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ asset('bundles/hmfpsearchtool/styles/search.css') }}">
{% endblock %}

{% block body %}
  {#
    The skip link is the first focusable thing on the page. Someone navigating
    by keyboard lands on it before the filters, so they can jump straight to the
    results instead of tabbing through every dropdown on their way there — which
    on a refinement page is the difference between usable and exhausting.

    It is visually hidden until focused, not display:none — a hidden element
    cannot receive focus, so display:none would remove it for everyone.
  #}
  <a class="skip-link" href="#search-results">Skip to results</a>

  <header class="site-header">
    <p class="site-brand"><a href="{{ path('hmfp_search_tool_search') }}">HMFP provider search</a></p>
  </header>

  <main id="main">
    {#
      Flash messages after a redirect, for EVERY page on this layout.

      Lives here rather than in each template because a controller that
      redirects with a flash has no way of knowing whether the page it lands on
      bothers to show it. When only profile/edit rendered them, every message on
      the claim pages — "check your email", "invalid security token", "already
      claimed" — was set, stored, and silently thrown away, so a refused form
      post looked exactly like the page reloading.

      Inside <main>, above the content, so that:
        - the skip link and the "main" landmark both land on it first, and
        - it is the first thing read after the page heading area, not buried
          under a form the person has already filled in once.

      Wrapped in its own block so a page with a genuinely different need can
      override it — but the default should be "shown", never "forgotten".
    #}
    {% block flashes %}
      {#
        Read the flash bag ONCE. app.flashes() empties the bag as it reads it,
        so checking "is not empty" with one call and looping with a second
        would consume the messages in the check and leave the loop with nothing.
      #}
      {% set flashes = app.flashes(['success', 'error', 'notice']) %}

      {#
        A visible word for each type, so the meaning never depends on colour
        alone (red/green is exactly the pair that collapses for colour-blind
        users).

        "Done" rather than edit's old "Saved": this layout serves the claim
        flow too, and "Saved: Check your email…" would read as nonsense.
      #}
      {% set flashLabels = {success: 'Done', error: 'Error', notice: 'Note'} %}

      {#
        role="alert" for errors (announced straight away by screen readers,
        even on page load), role="status" for the rest (polite — waits for a
        pause in whatever is being read).
      #}
      {% for type, messages in flashes %}
        {% for message in messages %}
          <p class="flash flash--{{ type }}" role="{{ type == 'error' ? 'alert' : 'status' }}">
            <strong class="flash__label">{{ flashLabels[type] }}:</strong> {{ message }}
          </p>
        {% endfor %}
      {% endfor %}
    {% endblock %}

    {% block content %}{% endblock %}
  </main>

  <footer class="site-footer">
    <p>Provider information is supplied by the hospital and refreshed on each import.</p>
  </footer>
{% endblock %}
